While watching the order book lately, I noticed many people are still burning themselves out on those painfully annoying airdrop interaction chores. But the eyes of people who truly know the game were already shifting toward liquidity allocation and the TGE battle. This GRVT move has a pretty interesting tempo: the newly opened token registration window directly puts a multiple-choice question on the table—do you just want to secure a quick win, or are you willing to lock up and make a long-term bet for that so-called “multiplier plan”? Honestly, this kind of approach that hands the power of capital-game decisions to users really does show a bit of “finance-grade” flavor in today’s decentralized derivatives arena. #BTC #ETH 

I’m not going to hype anyone with promises, but with this whole set of gameplay centered around TGE, it’s not just a simple token distribution—it directly quantifies participants’ liquidity expectations and the time cost of participating. Compared with those junk projects that dump the moment they list, the logic of obtaining higher weighted allocations through delayed distribution actually fits the appetite of institutional traders. Now that we have the progress on Mainnet Beta, plus the earlier groundwork along the compliance path, it’s enough to show this isn’t a chess game just meant to prop up a few retail users. The market’s reality will speak for itself—don’t just look at the community debating multiplier coefficients right now. What’s really worth considering is the real settlement efficiency under that ZK architecture and the transparency of on-chain assets, because that’s the core moat for us messing around with Perps in the crypto world. And as always: safety first—don’t use hot money to fill pits with no bottom you can’t even see. But this kind of hard-core project that strikes a balance between compliance and performance really is worth keeping under observation—just watch the TGE execution steps that come next.

